#: ../../Introduction/Python_Tutorial.rst:113
msgid "Units"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Introduction/Python_Tutorial.rst:115
msgid "Engineering requires calculations with units. Prior to modern computer languages engineers used paper and pencil, slide rules, calculators, and more recently spreadsheets to do calculations. All of these methods are prone to calculation errors because units aren't handled as an essential part of each value. Spreadsheets are especially notorious for calculation errors because unit conversions are buried in formulas that are hidden in the cells."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Introduction/Python_Tutorial.rst:117
msgid "Operations on values with units follow very clear algebraic rules and thus units can be attached to numerical values and carried through math operations. This capability is implemented in Python using `Pint <https://pint.readthedocs.io/en/latest/>`_ . The Pint package includes a host of units and prefixes (such as :math:`\\mu` for :math:`10^{-6}`). As you master using Python and Pint you will say goodbye to mindless unit conversions forever!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Introduction/Python_Tutorial.rst:119
msgid "Environmental engineers historically described surface loading rates for clarifiers using units of gal/min per square foot. How fast is :math:`\\frac{1 gpm}{ft^2}` in :math:`\\frac{mm}{s}`?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Introduction/Python_Tutorial.rst:127
msgid "The surface loading rate is 0.6791 millimeter / second"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Introduction/Python_Tutorial.rst:129
msgid "After reducing the number of significant digits to 2 we obtain: The surface loading rate is 0.68 millimeter / second"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Introduction/Python_Tutorial.rst:132
msgid "How long does it take to stop a car that is initially traveling at 60 mph if the coefficient of friction is 0.5?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Introduction/Python_Tutorial.rst:143
msgid "The time to stop the car is 120 mile / hour / standard_gravity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Introduction/Python_Tutorial.rst:145
msgid "We add the .to_base_units() directive to force pint to simplify the units."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Introduction/Python_Tutorial.rst:147
msgid "The time to stop the car is 5.47 second"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Introduction/Python_Tutorial.rst:149
msgid "Many functions written in Python do not yet handle units and thus it is sometimes necessary to remove the units. Examples include graphs (althougth units might be coming to matplotlib), SciPy functions, and the NumPy functions used to populate arrays. For these cases you can strip the units off a number using the .magnitude method. Be careful to make sure you know what the units are before you remove them otherwise you may be confused by the results!"
msgstr ""

